\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces What the $723\times $ ambiguity actually costs. Each curve is the same LMSR anchored at the observed mid of $0.035$, differing only in which side of the book $b$ was calibrated from. On the bid-side calibration a retail-sized order moves the synthetic market across its whole range; on the ask-side calibration no realistic trade moves it at all. The simulation's entire character is determined by a choice the data does not settle.}}{12}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bimpact}{{9}{12}{What the $723\times $ ambiguity actually costs. Each curve is the same LMSR anchored at the observed mid of $0.035$, differing only in which side of the book $b$ was calibrated from. On the bid-side calibration a retail-sized order moves the synthetic market across its whole range; on the ask-side calibration no realistic trade moves it at all. The simulation's entire character is determined by a choice the data does not settle}{figure.caption.14}{}}
